{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And the boys grew.</b> Rashi's explanation: once they became thirteen years old etc. — that until that point their father was occupied with them, rebuking them and teaching them the straight path. As stated in Bereshit Rabbah: a person must be occupied with his son until thirteen years; from then on he says, 'Blessed be the Makom who released me from the punishment of this one.' And if you ask: they must perforce have been fifteen years old at that point — how so? At that point Abraham died, so that he would not see Esau going out to bad culture, and Abraham was a hundred years old when Isaac was born to him, and Abraham lived a hundred and seventy-five years — so Isaac was seventy-five when Abraham his father died. Subtract from them the sixty years of Isaac when he fathered them, and there remain fifteen years from when they were born until Abraham died. Rather, it is found in the Aggadah that he did his evil deeds in secret for two years, and therefore those two years were not counted to him." "<b>A man knowing hunting.</b> His craft was through trickery, for hunting of beast and bird is not without deceit. And since it is about to say shortly, 'bring me game,' he preceded and taught you here that Esau was a man of hunting." "<b>Dwelling in tents.</b> According to its plain sense: tents of sheep, like his father — dwelling in tents and herding. And since it is about to say that he herded the sheep of Laban, he preceded and taught you that his trade was to herd sheep."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:57.773335397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}